>
  <h2>What a construction letter of intent is for</h2>
  <p>
    Subcontracts take time. The GC’s subcontract form has to be filled in, the exhibits assembled,
    insurance reviewed and signatures collected — while the schedule keeps moving. A letter of
    intent bridges that gap. It tells the subcontractor it has the job at the agreed price, so it
    can hold its number and its crews, and it can release the few things that cannot wait:
    submittals, shop drawings and orders for long-lead equipment. What it should not do is become
    the contract by accident.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the subcontractor, the letter arrives at the end of the bid process described in
    <a href="/blog/how-to-create-construction-bid/">how to bid a construction job</a> — and the first
    thing to check is that its scope and price match the proposal you submitted.
  </p>

  <h2>Binding and non-binding parts</h2>
  <p>
    The template separates the two on purpose. Paragraph 1 says the letter is a statement of intent
    and does not bind either side to sign the subcontract. Paragraph 2 is the part that is meant to
    bind: the GC pays for the early work it released, at documented cost plus the bid markup, up to
    the not-to-exceed amount, whether or not the subcontract is signed — and the sub hands over the
    work product and materials paid for. Paragraph 3 makes the signed subcontract supersede the
    letter and credits early-work payments against the price. Paragraph 4 lets the letter lapse on
    the dates stated, so an award does not hang open for months.
  </p>

  <h2>Letter of intent, subcontract, notice to proceed</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Document</th><th>When</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr
        ><td><strong>Letter of intent</strong></td><td>After the award, before the subcontract</td
        ><td>States the intended scope and price, releases capped early work, sets deadlines</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><strong>Subcontract</strong></td><td>Once terms are agreed</td><td
          >The binding contract: scope, price, payment, changes, insurance, lien waivers</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><strong>Notice to proceed</strong></td><td>After the subcontract is signed</td><td
          >Starts the contract time and confirms the conditions are met</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    For the subcontractor, the letter is also a checkpoint. Read the scope against your own bid and
    <a href="/templates/construction-bid-proposal/">proposal</a> before you countersign: exclusions you
    listed in the bid should appear in the letter, because the GC’s subcontract will be drafted from it.
    If the letter leaves out an exclusion or rounds the price, fix it now — it is far easier than fixing
    it in the subcontract.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example</h2>
  <p>
    In the completed example, Brightline Builders writes to Summit Mechanical on May 11, 2026 about
    the Harbor Point Medical Office, Building B. It awards the complete mechanical and plumbing
    scope per Summit’s April 24 bid for a lump sum of $486,200, and releases $38,000 of early work:
    equipment submittals and shop drawings, the purchase order deposit for the four rooftop units,
    and field verification of slab penetrations — no site work. The letter must be accepted by May
    15 and the subcontract signed by May 18; work is expected to start June 1 and reach substantial
    completion November 18. Summit countersigns on May 12. The subcontract that follows is the
    <a href="/templates/subcontractor-agreement/">subcontractor agreement</a> example, and the start is
    confirmed by the <a href="/templates/notice-to-proceed/">notice to proceed</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No cap on early work, so the GC’s exposure is unlimited if the deal falls apart.</li>
    <li>
      A vague scope that does not match the bid, the <a href="/templates/scope-of-work/"
        >scope of work</a
      >, or the subcontract that follows.
    </li>
    <li>
      Language that reads like a contract — “you are hereby awarded and shall perform” — with no
      intent-only clause.
    </li>
    <li>Starting site work on a letter that only released submittals.</li>
    <li>No expiration date, leaving the sub holding its price indefinitely.</li>
    <li>Not crediting early-work payments against the subcontract price.</li>
  </ul>
</TemplatePage>
